ationalism in East Asia  and I think that's that's another reason  and I think yet another reason is gender  that is there's baseball but women are  not allowed to play baseball they play  softball and actually Japanese women's  softball team has been world champions  it's quite good but it's not played much  in schools soccer is the one major sport  where women can play women's T in the  Japanese women's soccer team won the the  Women's World Cup this year they're  really powerful and so within the  country I think soccer is becoming  popular among the younger generation for  for also because girls can play soccer  in school and girls now have role models  among the elite women soccer players and  the Women's World Cup is becoming much  more of a of a high visible sort of  global event so I think you know  baseball and sumo were Japan's 20th  century sports but there's a strong  likelihood that soccer is going to be  Japan's 21st century sport you mentioned  earlier that some of the baseball  players would go to watch American  football games and took back the  cheering to the baseball games I am  curious to know if you have any thoughts  as to do they play football in Japan at  all and if they do why do you think it  hasn't taken off to the extent that  baseball has well it hasn't taken off  because baseball smothered all sports  not just American football it is a  popular sport at certain in certain  university leagues that is it remained  like rugby rugby is also popular like it  is in the u.s. in university clubs and  one interesting feature of sports in  Japan is that corporations have played a  much more important role in sponsoring  sports and organizing sports teams than  in the US or in Europe so all the major  corporations from the throughout the  second half of the 20th century had  baseball teams and basketball teams  and some American football teams for  their employees and they would actually  recruit employees to play on their  sports teams and there were  inter-corporate leagues so that like IBM  and Ford and Chevrolet would play would  have a basketball league or an ice  hockey league or swimming where they had  a wide range of sports and so Japan's I  mean things like the Olympic development  in Japan was largely due to and fed by  this corporate investment in sports and  sports teams and so things like rugby  and American football have found have  survived as niche sports sports in these  elite universities and in some of these  big corporations but by and large big  kids get channeled into sumo and the  rest of them get channeled into baseball  when I think of Japan and sports a lot  of times I go to the martial arts and  you had mentioned judo how is that  what's the status of judo in Japan today  well the ironic thing is do those an  individual sport and baseball is a team  sport and we always think of the  Japanese is very collectivist very group  but back in the 1870s 1880s the Ministry  of educators knew Ministry of Education  looked around and said we have a problem  in our school system our kids are not  not getting physically strong they're  weak we need to do something and and  they said we have a choice we can either  encourage judo and martial arts or we  can encourage some Western sports and  said we don't want these Japanese  they're too individualistic we want the  American sports in the in the British  sports because they teach teamplay and  so judo and the other martial arts  actually were not as encouraged in Japan  in the school system and as popular  pursuits but judo interestingly as  Japan's first global sport that is judo  from the 1920s there were a couple of  the impact the fellow who  in effect created invented judo out of a  number of different indigenous martial  arts traditions then went to the United  States it went to South America went to  Europe and brought judo so judo traveled  really around the world from the 1920s  on as Japan's contribution to the 20th  century sports inventory and the key  moment in the moment that really shocked  the Japanese was in 1964 when the Tokyo  Olympics were held that first time the  Olympics ever came to Asia and Tokyo  this was Tokyo Japan's coming-out party  after World War two they were going to  demonstrate that they had recovered that  they were a normal nation and they had a  couple of star judo players in the in  the the heavyweight or the the open  weight category the Japanese star lost  to a dutch judo master and this shocked  the Japanese in their own sport and all  it did was to say the judo had become  fully globalized at that point so judo  exists in Japan but the the the  international judo organization is not  in Japan it's in Europe and judo is is  much more of a global sport now because  of its basically Japan's gift to 20th  century sport what do you think is the  future of sports in Japan well it's hard  to predict I mean certainly Japan China  East Asia is the most dynamic sports  market in the world now in terms of  sports marketing in terms of sports  promotion and you look at soccer and you  look at the Olympics you look at golf  and you look at a number of a track and  field even so in East Asia generally it  will be probably the most dynamic growth  area for sports in the next couple  decades and the Japanese consume sports  as as avidly as people anywhere they  watch baseball although increasingly  they're watching our baseball rather  than their own baseball they're  certainly watching soccer from all over  the world sports that aren't that  important in the u.s. like auto racing  I'm very popular in Japan so it's part  of the International f1 auto racing  circuit horse racing is popular in Japan  so they've connected in ways with sports  around the world that actually don't  necessarily mean that sports will grow  in Japan but certainly sports  consciousness and sports markets and  sports enthusiasm and sports nationalism  for better or worse will be important in  Japan for 